  "response": "The `crimes scan` finding for `src/assets/check-icon.png` (crime_00014, \"Icon-Sized Raster\") quotes the dimensions verbatim as:\n\n> \"dimensions: 32 × 32 px (≤ 64 threshold both sides)\"\n> \"format: png\"\n\nA new SVG indicator is a better starting point than another 32×32 PNG because at icon dimensions the raster is locked to a single resolution — the existing PNG renders crisply only at exactly 32×32 device pixels, and degrades on any Retina/HiDPI display (2×, 3×) or anywhere the indicator is scaled up. SVG ships the same geometry at every DPI, scales cleanly across the breakpoints a status indicator will inevitably be reused at, and almost always weighs less on the wire than a 32×32 PNG once you account for PNG header/IDAT overhead. Copying the PNG pattern just propagates a defect the scanner has already flagged; starting from SVG avoids inheriting it for the new indicator.",
